nces among the different treatment groups should be equal (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nANOVA is applicable when comparing the means of three or more groups. It is particularly useful when:\n- The experimental design involves one categorical independent variable with multiple levels.\n- The researcher aims to understand the effect of different treatments on a continuous response variable.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure for single-factor ANOVA involves the following steps:\n1. Formulate the null hypothesis (\\( H_0 \\)): All group means are equal.\n2. Calculate the F-statistic, which is the ratio of the variance between the group means to the variance within the groups.\n3. Compare the calculated F-statistic to the critical value from the F-distribution to determine whether to reject the null hypothesis.\n4. If significant differences are found, post-hoc tests (e.g., Tukey's HSD) may be conducted to identify which specific groups differ.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- ANOVA allows for the comparison of multiple groups simultaneously, reducing the risk of Type I error associated with multiple t-tests.\n- It provides a clear framework for testing hypotheses about group means.\n- The method is robust to violations of normality when sample sizes are large due to the Central Limit Theorem.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- ANOVA does not indicate which specific groups are different; post-hoc tests are required for this purpose.\n- It is sensitive to violations of the homogeneity of variances assumption, which can lead to incorrect conclusions.\n- The interpretation of results can be complex, especially in the presence of interactions in multifactor designs.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated methods include:\n- Two-way ANOVA, which examines the effects of two factors simultaneously.\n- MANOVA (Multivariate Analysis of Variance), which extends ANOVA to multiple dependent